'''Wenzhou pig intestines rice noodle soup''' ({{zh|c=温州猪脏粉|p=Wēnzhōu zhūzàngfěn}}) is a noodle soup dish made with rice noodles, pig intestine and duck or pig blood. It comes from [[Wenzhou]], in [[Zhejiang]], China. It can be eaten at breakfast or throughout the day.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Wenzhou, a paradise of delicacies!|url=http://ct.zj.gov.cn/art/2019/8/19/art_1657329_37153398.html|access-date=2021-04-08|website=ct.zj.gov.cn}}</ref>

Intestines are cooked in aromatics such as [[rice wine]], soy sauce, ginger, [[soybean paste]], or garlic<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.chayedan.com/en/show/82020.html|title=Wenzhou pig dirty powder|website=www.chayedan.com|language=en|access-date=2017-04-15}}</ref> until softened. Blood is added in a congealed form (such as [[pig blood curd]]) along with noodles, and the dish is finished with scallions.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web|title=Sampling culinary classics from Wenzhou|url=http://www.ecns.cn/2015/11-05/187315.shtml|access-date=2021-04-08|website=www.ecns.cn}}</ref>

In an effort to give consistent English names to 50 popular Wenzhou dishes, Zhejiang Vocational and Technical College of Industry and Trade called it "Rice Rolls with Boiled Pig's Intestines" in 2008.<ref>{{Cite web|title=50道温州小吃有"洋名"了_新闻中心_新浪网|url=http://news.sina.com.cn/s/2008-04-22/092513775345s.shtml|access-date=2021-04-08|website=news.sina.com.cn|language=zh}}</ref>

As people from Wenzhou moved out of the city, they also introduced this dish to other regions.<ref name=":0" /> However, as the region develops and people in Wenzhou become more affluent, fewer people have been making authentic Wenzhou pig intestines rice noodle soup.{{Citation needed|date=October 2020}}
